[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Most people have four or five pictures of their AI influencer, and no idea how to get from there, to a real dataset. And in this video I show you the exact system we use, to turn a handful of pictures into a complete, training-ready-to-use dataset. No matter if you have five Instagram shots, or just one single face picture.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the dataset does not care, what you train it on later. That part is completely up to you. And everything we see today, the full prompt system, and the ComfyUI workflow, you get complete for free, at the end of this video.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, let\'s get straight into it. This is Lina, our AI influencer for this video. And these four pictures here, are everything we start with. One clean studio shot of her face, and three random phone pictures. And if you have an AI influencer, you probably have exactly that.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And whatever you build with Lina, it all comes out of one folder. But four pictures are not the dataset. Here\'s the part almost nobody talks about. You never generate everything out of these four pictures.'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You start with her face, and every step after that you use what you just made. This is a finished dataset. And this is not a folder with nice pictures of her, this is one whole person, from every side. The first part here is her face from every angle.'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And these are not nice portraits, this is the geometry of her face in every position a camera can stand And these are her expressions. Smiling, mouth open, eyes closed, and looking away. And then it comes to her body.'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Upper body from every side, and after that the full body from every side. And at the end comes the real-life pic. Just normal pictures of her, in a real room to add the hyperrealistic instagram effekt, to the dataset. This is a whole dataset. Around 30 pictures, and'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'every single one of them is doing a job. And if one of these parts is missing, you will feel it later, in every image you ever make with her. And you don\'t need hundreds of pictures for that. What matters is that every part is covered.'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And if you train LoRA on this later... 25 to 36 good ones, beat 75 messy ones, every time. And before we go into the prompts, one quick thing. Everything in this video is free and at the end you get the whole ComfyUI workflow and prompt system on top.'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So the only thing I want from you, is a like and a nice comment. And if you are really serious about building AI influencers, and you want a whole path, not just one piece of it then come over to our skool community.'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That is where we go way deeper than I can ever go here on YouTube. Okey, now every picture in the dataset comes from one prompt. And there\'s one rule for all of them. Get this one wrong, and nothing else will save you. These are the prompts for her face. And I want you to look one thing.'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Look at her hair color. There is none. Not in this one, and not in any of the prompts. Because identity never goes into the prompt. The pictures say who she is. The prompt only says where she is, and how it is shot.'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The moment you write blonde hair into a prompt that prompt only works for blondes. Leave it out, and the same prompt builds a dataset for every character you will ever make. And there\'s exactly one exception'

[02:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you can see it right here. Keep the same eye color as in the reference images. This is not a description. That points back at your own pictures. Some models drift by the eye color, so we lock it in. And that is the difference that keeps it save for every AI Influencer.'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Because the lock never says what her eyes look like, it only says, don\'t change them. And everything here comes out of that one studio picture of her face. So if you don\'t have one yet that is the first thing you make, and you make clean'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with nothing else in the frame. Now let\'s look at her face, because her face has to teach two things. The first one are the angles, so who she is from every side. And there her face stays neutral, because only the camera moves.'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And The second one are her expressions, so what her face can actually do. And there the cameras then front out, because now only her face moves. But never both in the same picture.'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Because you have to be able to see what changed between the shots. If the camera moves and her face moves at the same time you cannot tell if the mouth looks different because of the angle, or because of she is smiling.'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And one quick tip on the background take grey and not white, because white flattens the skin and that is where plastic look dark. And there\'s one more thing but that one does not go into the dataset. That is one single picture with all her expressions in a grid'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and we only build that when we want to make videos with her so the video model has your whole range in one reference. And that, is her face done, and these pictures are now our references for the body.'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now her body, and this is where it gets interesting. And Lina already has body pictures. But if all you have is a face then there is no body yet and the image model just invents one. And here comes the most important part of this whole video.'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The first body picture you accept, is your body from now on. Everything after that has to match it so take your time with this one. And from there we lock it. in. Same proportions as in the reference pictures, in every single prompt'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because a lot of models make her smaller or bigger on their own, without asking. And sometimes the model still does its own thing, and then you just run it one more time. And that same lock also works the other way around'

[04:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so you can make her curvier, or slimmer or a lot more than that. But this is a thing for our skool community not for YouTube, if you know what I mean. Then we turn her all the way around, from the front to the back.'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the outfit stays the same the whole way, because otherwise it\'s not a rotation it is just five different photos. And then the same positions again, only framed closer. Because later you will want her in a full body shot'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and in a tight one and she has to hold up and both. And that is her body done and with that we have her completely, from every side. And now we finally take her out of the studio. and that is exactly what makes an AI girl look like an AI girl.'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And these are the pictures you probably already have a few of, because that is what Instagram photos are. What you were missing the whole time, is the studio. So now we just take normal Instagram pictures of her'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and what matters here is not the pose, it is how the picture was taken. And you really want to mix in here. Blurry phone shots and razor-sharp ones, selfies and normal pictures some of them with the flash on, some in daylight, and some'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in a dark room at 9 to give the dataset a bit variation. Basically exactly what a normal Instagram girl has on her feed. And here is where a lot of people mess up, because they make these way too nice. A picture that looks planned is just a studio again, with a bedroom behind it.'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But cheap-looking is not the same as broken, and you still have to be able to tell that it\'s her. And if you cannot tell, then it does not go in the dataset, of course because if you train on this later, the LoRA just copies what you gave in.'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
